WEBVTT

00:00:03.778 --> 00:00:11.538
A notice or form is often the only way a person learns what the
Disability Services Division needs from them, and by when.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:17.471
If they cannot read it, use it or trust it, the decision behind it
may never reach them fairly.

00:00:17.593 --> 00:00:21.360
On the Practice page, scroll to Review before it goes out.

00:00:21.482 --> 00:00:24.223
You will see two guided practices.

00:00:24.346 --> 00:00:29.974
One is for reviewing someone else's draft, and the other is for
checking something you wrote yourself.

00:00:30.097 --> 00:00:34.219
You will use each of them here, along with the plain language
checklist.

00:00:34.786 --> 00:00:39.263
Open Reviewing a draft for equity, access, and plain language.

00:00:39.386 --> 00:00:44.834
The opening tells you what you will have at the end: a review record
for one specific draft.

00:00:44.957 --> 00:00:52.577
It captures what the draft asks of people, who may be excluded, your
access and language findings, the sources behind each claim, the

00:00:52.577 --> 00:00:55.799
changes you suggest and what remains for the owner.

00:00:55.921 --> 00:01:03.703
Bring a real draft, but keep confidential, predecisional or personal
material out of your notes, and work in a tool you already use.

00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:08.181
The path is laid out as Six steps to work through in order.

00:01:08.303 --> 00:01:16.507
You begin with Examine the draft, recording what it asks people to
do, where access or language may create a barrier, and the evidence

00:01:16.507 --> 00:01:17.848
for each finding.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:25.582
If the draft is aimed at particular Minnesota communities, the
second step points you to the relevant brief for naming, language

00:01:25.582 --> 00:01:26.537
and access.

00:01:26.659 --> 00:01:34.497
The third step, Choose practical resources, links to the
plain-language checklist, the language access checklist and a set of

00:01:34.497 --> 00:01:36.616
journey and burden questions.

00:01:36.738 --> 00:01:38.993
Let's open the first of those.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:44.833
The Plain language and accessible documents checklist opens with why
this matters.

00:01:44.956 --> 00:01:51.300
If a person cannot read or use a notice, they may not be able to
understand or appeal the decision.

00:01:51.422 --> 00:01:58.102
Under Use the checklist, aim for sentences of fifteen to twenty
words, one idea each.

00:01:58.224 --> 00:02:01.813
Use everyday words and say who does what by when.

00:02:01.936 --> 00:02:06.008
Write link text that describes the destination, never click here.

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:12.562
Describe important images in alternative text, and make sure error
messages on a form explain what to fix.

00:02:12.684 --> 00:02:19.430
Then run the built-in accessibility checker and ask someone outside
the team to describe what the document means to them.

00:02:19.997 --> 00:02:25.134
Back on the practice, the Draft review record shows what a complete
review covers.

00:02:25.257 --> 00:02:31.257
You note what the draft asks people to do, including steps,
documents, deadlines and return methods.

00:02:31.379 --> 00:02:38.168
You consider who may be excluded or burdened, by language,
disability, technology, time, trust or place.

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:42.590
You label the source of each claim and flag anything cited from
memory.

00:02:42.712 --> 00:02:46.479
You also note what you chose not to assume about who reads it.

00:02:46.601 --> 00:02:52.717
The record is for browsing and downloading only, so nothing you
write is collected or stored here.

00:02:53.283 --> 00:02:57.139
What a useful result looks like names the common shortcuts.

00:02:57.261 --> 00:03:00.486
Approved with no findings still needs work.

00:03:00.608 --> 00:03:07.752
So does a rewrite that changes the meaning without telling the
owner, and a reading-level number with no specific change attached.

00:03:07.874 --> 00:03:15.568
A result is ready when findings are specific and tied to who is
affected, sources are labeled, and the owner knows exactly what

00:03:15.568 --> 00:03:16.352
remains.

00:03:16.474 --> 00:03:21.109
The final step, Return it to the owner, makes the boundary clear.

00:03:21.231 --> 00:03:27.386
The author or content owner decides what changes, and your review
helps them without approving the draft.

00:03:27.952 --> 00:03:33.834
When the draft is your own, open Checking your own material for
access, assumptions, and tone.

00:03:33.957 --> 00:03:37.080
It asks you to read in three passes.

00:03:37.202 --> 00:03:40.436
The first covers access and plain language.

00:03:40.558 --> 00:03:48.304
The second asks what you assumed people already know, such as
deadlines, formats and places stated in full, and dates that avoid

00:03:48.304 --> 00:03:51.751
major religious observances or offer an alternative.

00:03:51.873 --> 00:03:58.929
The third pass is tone: invitational rather than punitive, built on
strengths and goals rather than deficits.

00:03:59.051 --> 00:04:05.662
A good result includes a sentence that now tells people how to
succeed instead of what happens if they fail.

00:04:06.229 --> 00:04:14.151
The last step, Ask a colleague to review it, is optional, and it
carries a simple truth: a second reader catches what the writer

00:04:14.151 --> 00:04:14.884
cannot.

00:04:15.007 --> 00:04:22.092
The draft review path gives that colleague a structure, and the
right office can help with language access or accessibility

00:04:22.092 --> 00:04:22.968
questions.

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:27.769
For your next step, choose one notice or form you expect to send
soon.

00:04:27.891 --> 00:04:35.558
Download the checklist from either practice, run a single pass this
week, and change one sentence so the person reading it knows exactly

00:04:35.558 --> 00:04:36.703
how to succeed.
